Source: vst-ace
Section: sound
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Connor McRann <cmcrann@protonmail.com>
# The :i386 entries below are what peload32 is built from, and they are not
# uniform in what their absence costs. FreeType is fatal -- peload/CMakeLists.txt
# stops the build without it. X11 is optional and costs the editor window.
# PipeWire and ALSA are queried as a single pkg-config call for both, so the
# ALSA entry alone would buy nothing: without libpipewire-0.3-dev:i386 beside
# it the query fails and peload32 is built render-only. That still serves the
# bridge, which carries audio over shared memory and lets the 64-bit host reach
# the sound server, but it silently drops standalone `peload32 --play`.
Build-Depends:
 debhelper-compat (= 13),
 cmake,
 pkg-config,
 libgtk-4-dev,
 qt6-base-dev,
 libasound2-dev,
 libpipewire-0.3-dev,
 libx11-dev,
 libcairo2-dev,
 libfreetype-dev,
 gcc-multilib,
 g++-multilib,
 libfreetype-dev:i386,
 libx11-dev:i386,
 libasound2-dev:i386,
 libpipewire-0.3-dev:i386
Standards-Version: 4.6.2
Homepage: https://github.com/spacestate1/VST-ace
Vcs-Git: https://github.com/spacestate1/VST-ace.git
Vcs-Browser: https://github.com/spacestate1/VST-ace

Package: vst-ace
Architecture: amd64
# Foreign because nothing about this package's interface is
# architecture-specific: the programs are found by path, not linked against.
# It was added for a separate vst-ace-i386 package to be able to depend on
# this one; that package is gone -- peload32 ships here now -- but the
# statement is still the true one, and it is what lets an i386 package on some
# other host satisfy a dependency on vst-ace.
Multi-Arch: foreign
Depends:
 ${shlibs:Depends},
 ${misc:Depends}
# The i386 runtime peload32 needs, as Suggests rather than Depends.
#
# Depends would make this package uninstallable on any host without
# `dpkg --add-architecture i386`, which is every host that only ever loads
# 64-bit plug-ins -- a dozen foreign-architecture libraries as the price of a
# feature they will not use. Suggests costs them nothing, and apt names the set
# when someone does want it. peload32 itself detects at run time whether these
# are present and says so plainly when they are not.
Suggests: xwayland,
 libc6:i386, libx11-6:i386, libfreetype6:i386,
 libasound2t64:i386, libpipewire-0.3-0t64:i386
Description: run Windows, macOS and Linux audio plug-ins natively, without Wine
 Audio plug-ins built for Windows, macOS and Linux, loaded and run as native
 code on Linux. Not emulation and not Wine: a PE loader with a Win32 subsystem
 underneath it, a Mach-O loader with an Objective-C runtime and a software
 Metal rasteriser, and a CFM/PEF interpreter for Classic Mac OS -- six hosts
 sharing one set of shims.
 .
 Two windows are included. pestudio (Qt6) is a plug-in browser and player: pick
 a folder, pick a plug-in, and get its programs, every exposed parameter, a
 playable keyboard, a pitch wheel, patch banks, a recorder, and the plug-in's
 own editor -- blitted for Windows plug-ins, embedded as an X11 child window
 for native Linux ones. dwstudio (GTK4) drives the reimplemented engines: a
 Korg DW-8000, a 4-op FM synth, a Juno-6 and sample kits.
 .
 The command line has the same hosts: va peload inspects and renders a plug-in
 without a window, and va play runs the DW-8000 engine with no plug-in loaded
 at all.
 .
 This package hosts 64-bit plug-ins, and carries peload32 for 32-bit Windows
 VST2 ones: the i386 loader the 64-bit hosts bridge to out of process. It needs
 the i386 runtime libraries in Suggests, which want dpkg --add-architecture
 i386 first; without them it says so rather than failing obscurely. No plug-in
 binaries are included -- the plug-ins these hosts load are their authors' own.
 .
 A few plug-ins import a Microsoft C or C++ runtime that cannot be stubbed,
 iostreams and locale objects having vtables and state of their own, and load
 only with the genuine DLL present. That is not ours to redistribute, so
 /usr/lib/vst-ace/runtime and .../runtime32 ship empty for it to be dropped
 into; the README there says what goes in them. Everything else works without.

# There is no second, i386 stanza here, and there is no vst-ace-i386 package
# any more. dh only builds the binary packages whose Architecture matches the
# host that `dpkg-buildpackage -b` runs on, so a hardcoded "Architecture: i386"
# stanza would be skipped on an amd64 build rather than cross-built, and
# build-deb.sh used to assemble that package by hand instead. The cost was that
# 32-bit support arrived only for users who knew to install a second thing.
# debian/rules installs peload32 into this package instead -- an i386 binary in
# an amd64 package, which dpkg is content with as long as dh_shlibdeps is kept
# away from it. See the override there.
